1075 #edb/ref 17592186432564 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432565 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432566 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432571 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1077 "selection" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432565 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432576 1079 #uuid "750328c3-c578-5e21-8518-8f7c29a90ca6" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432558 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432559 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1077 "field-3" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432558 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432577 1079 #uuid "a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432573 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432575 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432577 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1069 "q-334111" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1072 #uuid "10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432578 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432574 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1069 "q-334262" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1071 "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1072 #uuid "248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432579 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x).$ Notice that $\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+6x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x).$ Notice that $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{\\frac{1}{2}},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2})+6x(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2})-2\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ (-{x}^{1/2}+3{x}^{1/2}-2{x}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & {x}^{1/2} \\\\ -{x}^{-2} & \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-{x}^{1/2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}+{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432576 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1069 "q-334257" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1071 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1072 #uuid "8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432580 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}})+2x(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})-12(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432581 1062 #uuid "209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432581 1063 #edb/ref 17592186432579 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432582 1058 #edb/ref 17592186432583 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432582 1062 #uuid "5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432582 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308561 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432583 1058 #edb/ref 17592186432581 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432583 1062 #uuid "72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432583 1063 #edb/ref 17592186432580 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432584 1058 #edb/ref 17592186432585 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432584 1062 #uuid "a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432584 1063 #edb/ref 17592186335741 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432585 1058 #edb/ref 17592186432582 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432585 1062 #uuid "feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" 13194139534683 17592186045417 true] [17592186432585 1063 #edb/ref 17592186432578 13194139534683 1759218604541